Subject: experimental lazy JSON.parse markers and sequential materialization

What this does
  Adds an opt-in lazy JSON.parse representation for large documents. Normal
  object and dense-array slots contain marker values backed by one shared source
  and entry table. Scalars and nested containers are materialized directly from
  recorded source ranges; broad completion fills pending slots by the same
  direct entry path. Repeated object layouts can build later children directly
  from the first hashed shape.

Why we need it
  Large JSON payloads are often parsed and only partly inspected. The eager
  parser pays for the complete object graph even when most values are never
  read, while the earlier accessor prototype reparsed every small child
  container independently during broad traversal.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

Notes
  This is experimental and enabled only with QJS_JSON_LAZY=1. The marker
  representation is still heap allocated; an immediate JSValue marker can be
  evaluated after the materialization strategy is settled. QJS_JSON_LAZY_TRACE=1
  prints lifetime, fallback-parser, cursor, materialization, copy and slot-write
  counters. Complex strings and decimals still use counted temporary-parser
  fallbacks; integers, ASCII strings, booleans and null do not.

